Production rate=
	Normal production rate
	~	units/Week
	~	Production is now constrained by the available labor force.
	|

Hiring rate=
	Quit rate+Correction for labor
	~	person/Week
	~	The firm's hiring policy is to replace those workers who quit, modified by \
		a correction to bring the actual workforce into balance with the desired \
		workforce.
	|

Correction for labor=
	(Desired labor-Labor)/tcl
	~	person/Week
	~	Because of the delays in hiring new people, it takes 24 weeks to make \
		corrections to the workforce.
	|

Quit rate=
	Labor/ade
	~	person/Week
	~	Workers stay with the firm an average of 50 weeks (no-layoff policy).
	|

ade=
	50
	~	Week
	~	Average duration of employment
	|

Average labor productivity=
	20
	~	units/person/Week
	~	Average productivity is 20 units per worker per week and is quite \
		constant over time.
	|

Desired labor=
	Desired production rate/Average labor productivity
	~	person
	~	The desired workforce is determined by desired production and average \
		productivity.
	|

tcl=
	24
	~	Week
	~	Time to correct labor
	|

Normal production rate=
	Labor*Average labor productivity
	~	units/Week
	~	Union rules prevent the use of overtime or undertime.
	|

Labor= INTEG (
	+Hiring rate-Quit rate,
		50)
	~	person
	~	Initial labor = 1,000 / 20 = 50 persons (initial equilibrium).
	|

dic=
	4
	~	Week
	~	Desired inventory coverage
	|

Correction for inventory=
	(Desired inventory-Inventory)/tci
	~	units/Week
	~	The firm tries to correct discrepancies between desired and actual \
		inventory in eight weeks.
	|

Shipment rate=
	Order rate
	~	units/Week
	~	The firm supplies its customers from a stock of finished inventory, which \
		is generally sufficient to meet orders as they are received.
	|

Inventory= INTEG (
	+Production rate-Shipment rate,
		4000)
	~	units
	~	The initial inventory is four weeks' worth of shipments (1,000 x 4 = 4,000)
	|

Desired inventory=
	Average order rate*dic
	~	units
	~	Desired inventory is four weeks' worth ('dic') of anticipated shipments.
	|

Average order rate=
	SMOOTH(Order rate,taor)
	~	units/Week
	~	The firm forecast shipments by averaging past orders over an eight-week \
		period as a way of smoothing out any noise or lumpiness in demand.
	|

Order rate=
	1000*(1+step(0.1,10))
	~	units/Week
	~	In this model customer orders will equal a constant Order Rate (of 1,000 \
		units/week) until period 10, when orders step up by 10%, and then remain \
		at the higher rate.
	|

Desired production rate=
	Average order rate+Correction for inventory
	~	units/Week
	~	Desired production is determined by anticipated shipments, modified by a \
		correction to maintain inventory at the desired level.
	|

tci=
	8
	~	Week
	~	Time to correct Inventory
	|

taor=
	8
	~	Week
	~	Time to average order rate
	|

********************************************************
	.Control
********************************************************~
		Simulation Control Parameters
	|

FINAL TIME  = 100
	~	Week
	~	The final time for the simulation.
	|

INITIAL TIME  = 0
	~	Week
	~	The initial time for the simulation.
	|

SAVEPER  = 
        TIME STEP
	~	Week
	~	The frequency with which output is stored.
	|

TIME STEP  = 1
	~	Week
	~	The time step for the simulation.
	|

